"DanQing: An Up-to-Date Large-Scale Chinese Vision-Language Pre-training Dataset (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10305v1)\nciteturn30868view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn30867view3\",\"pattern\":\"Filtering\"}); Total lines: 402\nL66: To bridge the gap and advance Chinese multi-modal representation learning, we present DanQing, a high-quality dataset containing nearly 100 million Chinese image-text pairs collected after 2024. We begin with an initial corpus of approximately 1 billion pairs and implement a rigorous multi-stage filtration pipeline (Fig. cite65†1 ). First, we apply coarse-grained filtering based on safety, text length, and source reliability, retaining 45.4% of the raw data.\nL67: Next, we conduct fine-grained filtering along four dimensions: structure, quality, information density, and safety, at both the image and text levels. Leveraging parallel batch processing for computational efficiency, we filter out approximately 80% of the candidate pairs at this stage. We further provide an in-depth exploration and comprehensive analysis of DanQing, demonstrating that it surpasses existing large-scale Chinese datasets in both quality and temporal relevance.\nL68: Moreover, CLIP-based models pre-trained on DanQing consistently achieve state-of-the-art performance on downstream tasks such as zero-shot classification and cross-modal retrieval, and significantly improve the Chinese multimodal understanding capabilities of LLaVA-style MLLMs. The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:\nL69:   * •\nL70: \nL71: We develop an effective data filtering pipeline tailored for processing large-scale Chinese image-text pairs obtained from the Internet.\nL72: \nL73:   * •\nL74: \nL75: We release an up-to-date large-scale Chinese image-text dataset DanQing, which comprises nearly 100 million Chinese image-text pairs collected after 2024.\nL76: \nL77:   * •\nL78: \nL79: We conduct extensive experiments across multiple downstream tasks to demonstrate the effectiveness and scalability of DanQing.\nL80: ## 2 Related Work\nL81: ### 2.1 Vision-Language Pretraining\nL82: As a seminal work in vision-language pretraining, CLIP cite41†Radford et al. [2021a] has demonstrated exceptional zero-shot recognition and transfer capabilities. Building on this paradigm, recent studies have introduced various enhancements cite66†Yang et al. [2025] ; cite67†Gu et al. [2024] ; cite68†Wu et al. [2023] ; cite69†Hu et al. [2025a] ; cite70†Hu et al. [2025b] . For instance, SLIP cite71†Mu et al. [2022] integrates self-supervised learning with image-text pretraining to improve representation quality.\nL83: To mitigate the impact of noisy data, ALIP cite72†Yang et al. [2023] employs a gating mechanism for dynamic sample reweighting. DFN cite73†Fang et al. [2023] constructs new data filtering networks that induce state-of-the-art image-text datasets. MetaCLIP cite74†Xu et al. [2023] and MetaCLIP2 cite75†Chuang et al. [2025] leverage metadata derived from CLIP’s semantic concepts to curate balanced subsets from raw data pools. To further scale up the batch size, SigLIP cite60†Zhai et al.\nL84: [2023] and SigLIP2 cite76†Tschannen et al. [2025] adopt a sigmoid-based loss to replace the standard softmax, thereby eliminating the need for global normalization. In the context of Chinese vision-language pretraining, ChineseCLIP cite77†Yang et al. [2022] introduces a two-stage framework comprising locked-image tuning followed by contrastive tuning. Similarly, R2D2 cite63†Xie et al. [2023] enhances representation learning through a preranking-ranking strategy combined with bidirectional distillation.\nL85: Despite these advances, progress in Chinese vision-language pretraining remains limited due to the scarcity of large-scale Chinese image-text datasets.\nL86: ### 2.2 Large-Scale Image-Text Dataset\nL87: The impressive performance of CLIP cite41†Radford et al. [2021a] across downstream tasks is primarily attributed to the availability of massive, high-quality image-text data. To further advance model capabilities, numerous large-scale image-text pair datasets have been introduced in recent years (Tab. cite78†1 ). The YFCC100M cite62†Thomee et al. [2016] dataset provides a comprehensive record of photo and video sharing trends on Flickr from its inception in 2004 through early 2014. LAION400M cite79†Schuhmann et al.\nL88: [2021] comprises 400 million image-text pairs sourced from Common Crawl and has become a standard benchmark for vision-language pretraining. COYO-700M cite80†Byeon et al. [2022] collects approximately 10 billion image-alt-text pairs from HTML documents in Common Crawl (from October 2020 to August 2021), employing efficient image- and text-level filtering to remove uninformative pairs at minimal cost. To support data filtering research and benchmarking, DataComp cite57†Gadre et al.\nL89: [2023] assembled a pool of 12.8 billion image-text pairs for competition tracks, model training, and evaluation. However, these datasets are predominantly based on English image-text pairs, while large-scale Chinese image-text datasets remain scarce. To address this gap, the Wukong cite56†Gu et al. [2022a] dataset, comprising 100 million Chinese image-text pairs collected from the web, has been released. Taisu cite64†Liu et al.\nL90: [2022] further advances this effort by introducing an automatic filtering framework, resulting in a large-scale, high-quality Chinese multimodal dataset containing approximately 166 million images and 219 million Chinese captions. Leveraging user click-through rates and diverse textual information for each image, the Zero cite63†Xie et al. [2023] dataset offers 250 million images and 750 million corresponding Chinese texts, significantly advancing resources for Chinese vision-language pretraining.\nL91: Despite these advancements, the scale of existing Chinese image-text datasets remains limited, and their quality requires further improvement.\nL92: ## 3 DanQing Dataset\nL93: ### 3.1 Training Objective of DanQing\nL94: The DanQing dataset aims to enhance the Chinese embedding capabilities of CLIP-style models. To validate the efficacy of DanQing, we first use the image encoder $f_{v}(\\cdot)$ and text encoder $f_{t}(\\cdot)$ to map the image $x$ and text $y$ into normalized embeddings $\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$. While the standard CLIP objective cite41†Radford et al.\nL95: [2021a] aligns modalities via a symmetric cross-entropy loss over a batch of $B$ image-text pairs, its softmax normalization imposes a computational bottleneck at scale. To address this, we employ the SigLIP cite76†Tschannen et al. [2025] objective, which reformulates alignment as independent binary classification tasks using a sigmoid loss:\nL96:  | $$\\mathcal{L}=-\\sum_{i,j}\\left[\\mathbb{I}_{i=j}\\log\\sigma(s_{ij})+\\mathbb{I}_{i\\neq j}\\log(1-\\sigma(s_{ij}))\\right],$$  |\nL97: \nL98: where $s_{ij}=(\\mathbf{v}_{i}\\cdot\\mathbf{t}_{j})/\\tau+b$ is the scaled similarity with bias $b$.\nL99: \nL100: cite81†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of the DanQing dataset construction pipeline.\nL101: ### 3.2 Curation of DanQing\nL102: Data Collection. To curate a high-quality Chinese vision-language dataset, we first collect raw image-text pairs from the Common Crawl (2024–2025). The data collection is partitioned into seven batches and processed in parallel to ensure computational efficiency. By filtering for the “zho” language tag, we obtain an initial pool of approximately 1.05 billion pairs.\nL103: To mitigate the inherent noise in web-scale data, we implement a coarse-grained filtering pipeline based on three criteria: ① Content Safety: a lightweight 1M-parameter binary classifier cite82†Baidu AI Studio Community [2021a] filters unsafe content; ② Textual Constraints: only segments containing 5 to 60 words are retained; and ③ Source Reliability: pairs from a manually curated blacklist of low-quality websites are excluded. This filtering yields 706 million candidate pairs ($\\sim$67% retention).\nL104: Subsequent image downloading achieves a 67% success rate, resulting in a final collection of roughly 475 million high-quality image-text pairs.\nL105: Textual-Level Purification & Filtering. Prioritizing text-based pruning due to its lower computational overhead, we implement a multi-stage refinement pipeline across four dimensions: linguistic structure, text quality, information density, and safety. ① Linguistic Structure: We employ FastText cite83†Joulin et al. [2016] to identify and retain Chinese text, followed by OpenCC cite84†BYVoid [2024] to standardize all content into Simplified Chinese.\nL106: ② Text Quality: To ensure grammatical and lexical integrity, we discard samples that lack nouns or contain more than five [UNK] tokens after SigLIP tokenization cite76†Tschannen et al. [2025] . ③ Information Density: Following RealSyn cite40†Gu et al. [2025b] , we strip emojis and special characters, then apply an entropy-based semantic filter $H=-\\sum_{i}P(c_{i})\\log_{2}P(c_{i})$, where $P(c_{i})$ denotes the probability of token occurrence. We eliminate low-content captions with $H<6\\mathrm{e-}4$.\nL107: ④ Safety: A 20M-parameter NSFW detector cite85†Baidu AI Studio Community [2021b] and the Baidu DataBuilder service are utilized to filter out advertisements, sensitive political content, and territorial disputes. This stage reduces the corpus from 475 million to 397 million pairs, a 16.4% reduction that significantly enhances the signal-to-noise ratio for subsequent training.\nL108: Image-Level Filtering. To ensure visual integrity and content richness, we implement a multi-stage filtering pipeline focusing on four dimensions: visual fidelity, information density, image redundancy, and content safety. ① Visual Fidelity: We first remove irregular images by retaining only those with an aspect ratio between 1:3 and 3:1 and a shortest edge exceeding 100 pixels. To eliminate uninformative samples (e.g., solid colors), we prune images with a pixel intensity standard deviation $\\sigma<2$.\nL109: Furthermore, blurry images are mitigated by applying a Laplacian variance threshold $\\beta\\geq 1000$ computed via OpenCV cite86†team [2024] . ② Information Density: We quantify content complexity via image entropy $H=-\\sum_{i=0}^{255}P(i)\\log_{2}P(i)$, where $P(i)$ is the probability of pixel value $i$, and remove samples with $H<3$. ③ Image Redundancy: To minimize perceptual and semantic duplication, inspired by CLIP-CID cite66†Yang et al. [2025] , we use the Chinese-CLIP-L14 cite77†Yang et al.\nL110: [2022] to extract image embeddings and employ a Union-Find-based cite87†Tarjan [1975] clustering algorithm where images within a distance threshold of $\\beta=0.1$ are grouped. Within each cluster, we retain a single representative sample and prune the remainder to ensure dataset diversity. ④ Content Safety: We employ an 86M-parameter NSFW binary detection model cite88†S.L. [2025] to exclude pairs flagged with the highest risk level.\nL111: This comprehensive refinement reduces the dataset from 397M to 178M pairs, representing a 44.8% retention rate relative to the preceding stage.\nL112: Cross-Modal Filtering. Following LAION400M cite79†Schuhmann et al. [2021] , we leverage an expert model to further refine the dataset based on image-text alignment. Specifically, we compute similarity scores using Chinese-CLIP-L14 cite77†Yang et al. [2022] and retain pairs within the [1.06, 1.24] interval.\nL113: This thresholding strategy ensures high-quality alignment: scores below 1.06 indicate weak semantic correlation, while those exceeding 1.24 often correspond to images dominated by OCR text rather than descriptive content. This stage prunes 25 million pairs. The pipeline culminates in a curated dataset of approximately 100 million high-quality image-text pairs.\nL114: ### 3.3 Statistic of DanQing\nL115: \nL116: (a) Image Resolution Distribution.\nL117: \nL118: (b) Text Chinese words length.\nL119: \nL120: Figure 2: Overview of data characteristics in DanQing.\nL310: Low-textual images  | 352,204,550  | 66.36%  | 0.13%  | 33.64%\nL311: Blurry images  | 333,255,945  | 68.17%  | 5.38%  | 31.83%\nL312: Entropy  | 316,359,868  | 69.79%  | 5.07%  | 30.21%\nL313: Visual redundancy  | 186,019,602  | 82.23%  | 41.20%  | 17.77%\nL314: NSFW  | 178,601,215  | 82.94%  | 3.25%  | 17.06%\nL315: Cross-modality Filtering  | 154,293,590  | 85.26%  | 13.61%  | 14.74%\nL316: Cross-batch Visual Redundancy  | 99,892,381  | 90.46%  | 35.26%  | 9.54%\nL317: Final image-text pairs  | 99,892,381  | 90.46%  | –  | 9.54%\nL319: We further illustrate the statistical breakdown of our data construction pipeline in Tab. cite222†6 . Starting with an initial collection of approximately 1B raw image-text URLs, we apply a multi-stage refinement process, comprising coarse- and fine-grained filtering, cross-modality filtering, and deduplication. This pipeline ultimately yields nearly 100M high-quality image-text pairs (storage space occupies approximately 12TB), effectively filtering out 90% of the original noise to ensure data quality.\nL320: ## Appendix B Examples in DanQing Dataset\nL321: \nL322: cite223†Image: Refer to caption Figure 9: Visualization of image-text pairs in DanQing dataset.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAEQ-Bench: Measuring Empathy of Omni-Modal Large Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10513v1)\nciteturn30868view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn30867view0\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 679\nL90: To answer this question, we introduce AEQ-Bench, a benchmark designed to assess the empathic responsiveness and judgment capabilities of OLMs. It comprises 1,885 English instances, each pairing an audio utterance with a concise textual context. It overcomes the limitations of existing empathetic dialogue evaluations, which lack multimodality (see Fig. cite67†1 ) and parallel comparisons (over varying contexts and acoustic tones).\nL249: ## Limitations\nL250: \nL251: Our work provides an initial exploration into the EQ of OLMs, but it is subject to several limitations that suggest future research directions:\nL252: \nL253: Language and Cultural Scope: AEQ-Bench is constructed exclusively in English. Empathy is highly language- and culture-dependent, meaning the emotional cues, appropriate supportiveness strategies, and even the naturalness of delivery can vary drastically across different linguistic groups.\nL254: Coarse-Grained Metric: Our metrics use a coarse-grained categorical rating (\"Good,\" \"Fair,\" \"Poor\"). While evaluating audio quality using OLM judges has high consistency with coarse-grained metrics, the complexity of assessing the nuanced emotional intent and consistency of the model-generated audio output remains challenging.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCtD: Composition through Decompositionin Emergent Communication (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10169v1)\nciteturn30868view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn30867view1\",\"pattern\":\"3 \"}); Total lines: 750\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Emergent communication and coordination games L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Multi-target coordination games L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Communication Modes L20:     3. cite10†2.3 Evaluating Compositionality L21:   4. cite11†3 Composition through decomposition L22:     1. cite12†3.1 Two-step training regime L23:     2. cite13†3.2 Learning with a discrete codebook L24:     3. cite14†3.3 Training with multiple-loss function L25:   5. cite15†4 Experimental setup L26:     1. cite16†4.1 Data specifications L27:       1. cite17†Datasets L28:       2. cite18†Rule construction L29:       3. cite19†Data splits L30:     2. cite20†4.2 Communication baselines L31:     3. cite21†4.3 Training details L32:   6. cite22†5 Results L33:     1. cite23†5.1 Main results L34:       1. cite24†Decomposition (D) performance L35:       2. cite25†Composition through decomposition (CtD) performance L36:       3. cite26†Zero-shot (CtD-ZS) performance L37:       4. cite27†Composition without decomposition (C/D) performance L38:     2. cite28†5.2 Auxiliary and ablation results L39:       1. cite29†Learning with multiple targets L40:       2. cite30†Vocabulary size L41:       3. cite31†Codebook utilization and visualization L42:       4. cite32†Multi-loss optimization L43:   7. cite33†6 Related work L44:     1. cite34†The necessity of multiple targets: L45:     2. cite35†Codebook as a discrete communication channel L46:     3. cite36†Iterated Learning L47:   8. cite37†7 Conclusions L48:   9. cite38†References L49:   10. cite39†A Coordination game setups L50:     1. cite40†A.1 Game variants L51:       1. cite41†Ref game L52:       2. cite42†Recon game L53:       3. cite43†Diff game L54:       4. cite44†Mref game L55:     2. cite45†A.2 Communication modes L56:       1. cite46†Continuous communication L57:       2. cite47†Reinforcement learning L58:       3. cite48†Gumbel-softmax L59:       4. cite49†Quantized-communication L60:     3. cite50†A.3 Evaluation metrics L61:       1. cite51†Adjusted Mutual Information (AMI) L62:       2. cite52†Positional disentanglement (posdis) L63:       3. cite53†Bag-of-symbols disentanglement (bosdis) L64:       4. cite54†Context independence (CI) L65:       5. cite55†Concept Best Matching (CBM) L66:   11. cite56†B Datasets L67:     1. cite57†Thing (Carmeli et al., 2024) L68:     2. cite58†Shape (Kuhnle and Copestake, 2017) L69:     3. cite59†Mnist (LeCun et al., 1998) L70:     4. cite60†Coco (Lin et al., 2014b) L71:     5. cite61†Qrc L72:   12. cite62†C Detailed results L73:     1. cite63†C.1 Mref game results L74:     2. cite64†C.2 GS and QT CtD results L75:     3. cite65†C.3 Evaluating the compositionality metrics L76:     4. cite66†C.4 Diff game results L77:     5. cite67†C.5 Data mismatch between Decompose and Compose steps L78:     6. cite68†C.6 Qrc: a non-compositional dataset L79:   13. cite69†D Learning with multiple targets L152: ### 2.3 Evaluating Compositionality\nL153: A large body of work has attempted to characterize EC in light of natural language traits, such as compositionality (cite109†Hupkes et al., 2020 ; cite145†Chaabouni et al., 2020 ), systematic generalization (cite146†Vani et al., 2021 ), pragmatism (cite147†Andreas and Klein, 2016 ; cite148†Zaslavsky et al., 2021 ), and more.\nL156: Bag of Symbols Disentanglement (BOS) (cite145†Chaabouni et al., 2020 ) assesses the degree to which words in an EC language unambiguously correspond to NL concepts regardless of their position. Context Independence (CI) (cite152†Bogin et al., 2018 ) measures the alignment between EC words and NL concepts by examining their probabilistic associations across a set of EC messages and NL phrases.\nL157: Concept Best Matching (CBM) (cite153†Carmeli et al., 2024 ) quantifies the best match between a set of EC messages and a corresponding set of NL phrases. We provide more information on these metrics in Appendix cite40†A.1 .\nL158: ## 3 Composition through decomposition\nL159: \nL160: In this section, we present the CtD approach. We begin by outlining the two-step training regimen of CtD, followed by its utilization of a codebook to decompose the latent space into a discrete set of vectors. Finally, we detail the multi-loss optimization process used to concurrently optimize the vector representations of the codebook and the task’s success.\nL161: ### 3.1 Two-step training regime\nL162: The CtD approach is based on the idea of “Breaking down to build up”. To achieve that, CtD involves two sequential training steps. First, in the Decompose step, agents are trained to learn a codebook with distinct sets of concepts using a multi-target Single-Concept dataset. (Refer to Section cite8†2.1 for more details on multi-target games.) For example, to let the agent learn the concept Blue, we create a data sample with (at least) two targets: a Blue Circle and a Blue Square. See Figure cite123†1 (Left).\nL163: Second, in the Compose step, we train the sender to describe an image from a Composite-Phrase dataset by composing together a set of words from the learned codebook. For example, after learning the concepts Blue and Triangle during the Decompose step, the sender needs to compose a description Blue Triangle during the Compose step. See Figure cite123†1 (Right).\nL164: The two training steps differ in the data they use. Formally, each data sample $d_{k}\\in D$ is a quadruple $\\langle{\\mathbb{T}}^{s}_{k},{\\mathbb{D}}^{s}_{k},{\\mathbb{T}}^{r}_{k},{\\mathbb{D}}^{r}_{k}\\rangle$ composed from a set of the sender’s targets and distractors and the receiver’s targets and distractors, respectively. In the Decompose step, all targets share a phrase composed from exactly one concept (FVP).\nL165: Unlike cite122†Mu and Goodman (2021) , we do not use distractors in this step, demonstrating their redundancy for concept learning in a multi-target setup. In the Compose step, all targets share a phrase composed from a set of concepts (FVPs) with a predefined length, $l$. Importantly, the sender’s targets, ${\\mathbb{T}}^{s}_{k}$, need not be the same as the receiver’s ${\\mathbb{T}}^{r}_{k}$, but all targets must share the same phrase.\nL166: The number of targets and distractors may vary and is part of the configuration of each game.\nL167: Code-words are extracted from the codebook based on their similarity with the image representation $U^{S}$. During the Decompose step the top $l$ most similar vectors are extracted from the codebook, where $l$ is a dataset-dependent parameter. The sender then concatenates these $l$ vectors to form a bag-of-words message, $Z^{s}$.\nL168: ### 3.2 Learning with a discrete codebook\nL169: CtD uses a discrete codebook (cite121†Van Den Oord et al. (2017) ; cite154†Zheng and Vedaldi (2023) ) to encourage the sender to transmit disentangled image features or concepts. The codebook consists of a set of learnable vectors (code-words), each representing a discrete concept in latent space, which we term word. Discretization is achieved by replacing an input representation vector with the closest word in the learned codebook.\nL170: The words in the codebook are updated during training to best represent the distribution of data. A key advantage of using a codebook over other discretization methods is its ability to leverage a discrete latent space, learned during the Decompose step, for communicating compositional phrases during the Compose step. We provide a formal definition of the codebook in Appendix cite72†E .\nL171: ### 3.3 Training with multiple-loss function\nL172: We train the system end-to-end by minimizing the loss: ${\\mathcal{L}}={\\mathcal{L}}_{t}+\\beta_{1}{\\mathcal{L}}_{c}$. The task loss ${\\mathcal{L}}_{t}$ is game-specific and typically involves either cross-entropy (CE) for Mref games, binary cross-entropy (BCE) for Diff games, or mean squared error (MSE) for Recon games. Minimizing it leads to high accuracy on the game.\nL173: The codebook loss ${\\mathcal{L}}_{c}$ (cite155†Oord et al., 2018 ) is optimized by minimizing the MSE between the latent vectors $z$ and the code-words $w$,\nL174:  | $${\\mathcal{L}}_{c}=||\\mathrm{sg}[z]-w||^{2}_{2}+\\beta_{2}{||z-\\mathrm{sg}[w]||^{2}_{2}},$$  |  | (1)\nL175: \nL176: where $\\mathrm{sg}(\\cdot)$ denotes the stop gradient function, needed to overcome the discreteness of the codebook and $\\beta_{2}$ is a hyper-parameter that balances the two codebook loss components. Refer to Appendix cite78†G for more details.\nL177: The codebook loss encourages the sender’s output representation, $U^{S}$ (see Fig. cite138†2 ), to be close to the nearest word in the codebook. This is accomplished by utilizing the stop gradient function, which effectively employs a “straight-through” estimator. This mechanism allows gradients to flow through the quantization step during back-propagation (cite121†Van Den Oord et al., 2017 ). Minimizing the codebook loss often leads to high compositionality score as measured by the CBM and AMI metrics.\nL204: We compare our results with two communication baselines, Gumbel-softmax (GS) (cite118†Havrylov and Titov, 2017 ; cite117†Jang et al., 2017 ) and Quantized (QT) (cite119†Carmeli et al., 2023 ). See Section cite7†2 for details. We configured the GS vocabulary to match the number of concepts in each dataset. Additionally, for QT, we set the word length to enable the encoding of at least as many concepts as there are in the dataset.\nL205: As expected, the two-step training regime did not yield any benefits for GS and QT, so we do not report those results.\nL206: ### 4.3 Training details\nL207: The EC setup encompasses numerous configuration and hyper-parameters that can be adjusted during experiments. To ensure a fair comparison, we modify only the parameters being tested. Specifically, we maintain consistency in the architecture of the agents ($u_{\\theta}$ and $u_{\\phi}$ in Figure cite138†2 ) and network dimensions across all communication types. Furthermore, we select optimal vocabulary parameters for the different communication types.\nL208: Agent architectures, $u_{\\theta}$ and $u_{\\phi}$, vary slightly across games to optimize the encoding of images into latent vectors $U^{s}$ and $U^{r}$. Refer to Appendix cite78†G for more details on the configuration and training parameters and procedures.\nL209: ## 5 Results\nL210: \nL211: In this section, we present our experimental findings. We begin by outlining our primary results depicted in Figure cite160†3 . For detailed numerical results, please refer to Table cite161†4 in Appendix cite62†C . In Section cite28†5.2 , we provide supplementary analyses and results from ablation experiments. Results for additional configurations and experiments are provided in Appendices cite69†D and cite72†E . We discuss the limitations of the CtD approach in Appendix cite96†J L212: ### 5.1 Main results\nL213: \nL214: Figure 3: Accuracy and CBM results for the four compositional datasets (excluding the non-compositional Qrc) and the three communication modes (Gumbel-softmax in green, Quantized in red and Codebook in purple) for the Single-Concept dataset (Left) and the Composite-Phrase dataset (Right).\nL216: Analyzing agents’ performance on the Single-Concept dataset (Figure cite160†3 , Left) shows that codebook-based communication (indicated by purple letters in Figure cite160†3 and the ‘D’ rows in Table cite161†4 in Appendix cite62†C ) achieves perfect accuracy (ACC) and compositionality (AMI, POS, BOS, CI and CBM) across the Thing, Shape, and Mnist games. However, poorer results were observed for the Coco and non-compositional Qrc datasets (Table cite161†4 in Appendix cite62†C ), which we further investigate below.\nL284:   * Chaabouni et al. (2020) R. Chaabouni, E. Kharitonov, D. Bouchacourt, E. Dupoux, and M. Baroni Compositionality and generalization in emergent languages. In ACL, pp. 4427–4442. Cited by: cite201†§A.3 , cite202†§A.3 , cite203†§C.3 , cite177†§2.3 , cite184†§2.3 .\nL285:   * Choi et al. (2018) E. Choi, A. Lazaridou, and N. de Freitas Compositional obverter communication learning from raw visual input. In ICLR, Cited by: cite204†§A.1 , cite181†§H.3 , cite182†§H.3 , cite183†§H.3 , cite205†Appendix I , cite179†§2 , cite186†§6 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdjusted Similarity Measures and a Violation of Expectations (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10641v1)\nciteturn30868view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn30867view2\",\"pattern\":\"4 \"}); Total lines: 379\nL274: We further found that adjustment with respect to some models can produce adjusted indices in violation of the defining desired properties. These violations arise when distributions used in adjustment are data-driven. Permutation distributions, i.e., hypergeometric distributions, appear as special because while they are typically data-driven, they also fix observed properties and meet sufficient conditions for favorable adjustment properties as a result.\nL275: This frames the foundational work [cite27†Albatineh et al., 2006 ] as specific in its focus on the permutation distribution and further motivates modern [cite39†Gates and Ahn, 2017 ] and classic [cite33†Krippendorff, 1987 ] interest in the choice of models besides the permutation model.\nL276: Future theoretical and empirical work in this area will benefit from more precise attention to models used in adjustment. Where possible, consideration of models, indices, and normalizations which meet the constancy assumptions of Corollary cite54†3.5 , as by cite39†Gates and Ahn [2017] , would ensure favorable properties.\nL277: ### 4.1 Future Work\nL278: The treatment of index adjustment in this work presumes that favorable properties should hold exactly under the null distribution. In practice, it may be sufficient if these properties hold approximately. For example, Scott’s $\\pi$, which is the adjustment of $p$ under the data-driven independence model for 1 population with respect to a domain maximum, fails to satisfy constancy conditions and does not have mean 0 under the null distribution.\nL279: cite32†Scott [1955] provided a large sample characterization instead. Future work might investigate sufficient conditions for favorable asymptotic behavior when constancy conditions are not met. Clustering will supply a particularly interesting context for such investigation as not only may the number of labels vary under the null distribution, but the maximum possible number of labels may grow unbounded with sample size.\nL280: Extensions to fuzzy clustering with different null models [cite58†DeWolfe and Andrews, 2025 ] might also be considered.\nL281: ## Appendix A Proposition cite56†3.7 parts (3.), (4.), and (5.) allowing different conventions $c$ for when the maximal index value is expected.\nL282: In the context of the proof of Proposition cite56†3.7 , violation of properties (3.), (4.), and (5.), we consider the newly relaxed convention that when $\\text{E}_{M^{n}_{2,ind}}\\left[S(\\tilde{n})\\right]=S^{n}_{max}$, we assign fixed value $AS(n)=c$.\nL283: For reference, two a priori natural choices of $c$ would be $c=0$, as originally considered, suggesting no similarity beyond expectation when maximal similarity is expected, and $c=1$, suggesting perfect similarity beyond expectation when maximal similarity is expected as well as ensuring domain maximum $AS_{max}=1$.\n"
